# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Arrays", worked example "Incrementing a cell".
id: array-increment
title: Incrementing a cell
topic: Arrays
level: 2
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Arrays, "Incrementing a cell"
tags: arrays, array assignment, select-store

task:
  Calculate the weakest precondition of the statement for the
  postcondition $Q$ with the rule ARRAY-ASSIGN, in three steps:
  substitution, select-store, and definedness. Then use the consequence
  rule to decide whether the triple is valid.

pre: a[i] >= 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
post: a[i] > 0

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  a[i] := a[i] + 1     // i1
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of a[i] := a[i] + 1
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  The statement has the shape $a[I] := E$ with $I = i$ and
  $E = a[i] + 1$. ARRAY-ASSIGN substitutes the array $a$ in $Q$ with the
  updated array $a[i |-> a[i] + 1]$. It adds $def(a[i])$ for the write,
  and $def(a[i])$ again for the read in $E$. The $a$ inside $E$ is not
  replaced: it is the array before the write.
calc:
  wp(a[i] := a[i] + 1, Q)
  = (a[i] > 0)[a <- a[i |-> a[i] + 1]] && def(a[i]) && def(a[i])
  = (a[i] > 0)[a <- a[i |-> a[i] + 1]] && def(a[i])     // A && A is A
  = a[i |-> a[i] + 1][i] > 0 && def(a[i])               // the a of Q is replaced by a[i |-> a[i] + 1]
  = a[i] + 1 > 0 && def(a[i])                           // select-store: the read index i is the written index i, so the read gives the written value
  = a[i] >= 0 && def(a[i])                              // for integers, a[i] + 1 > 0 is a[i] >= 0
  = a[i] >= 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)                     // def(a[i]) is 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
set: R1 = a[i] >= 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)

step: P implies R1
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R1
text:
  $R1$ is the weakest precondition of the statement for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R1$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R1»
vc: a[i] >= 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) ==> a[i] >= 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
why:
  $P$ and $R1$ are the same formula.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  A test with the array $4, 0, 9$ and $i = 1$: position $1$ exists and
  $a[1] = 0$ is not negative, so $P$ holds. The statement sets $a[1]$ to
  $1$, and $1 > 0$.
